WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.680
Break one signaling receptor in a frog cell, without adding anything new.

2
00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:09.840
What will an animal cap become?

3
00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:15.830
Every experiment here uses the same tissue, the animal cap, cultured in salt

4
00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:16.830
solution.

5
00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:21.330
Left intact, it becomes epidermis and makes epidermal keratin.

6
00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:23.170
That is the control.

7
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:29.480
BMPs signal through pairs of receptor kinases, a type II and a type I.

8
00:00:30.433 --> 00:00:34.013
The type I receptor phosphorylates Smad1, 5 or 8.

9
00:00:34.753 --> 00:00:36.813
It moves to the nucleus with Smad4.

10
00:00:37.817 --> 00:00:40.237
Now remove one receptor's kinase domain.

11
00:00:41.177 --> 00:00:44.317
It still binds ligand and still pairs with normal partners.

12
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:49.280
But it cannot pass the signal on, so it jams the complexes it joins.

13
00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:54.770
This is a dominant negative: a broken copy that blocks the working copies.

14
00:00:55.833 --> 00:01:02.693
In 1994, Ali Hemmati-Brivanlou and Douglas Melton injected eggs with RNA for such a

15
00:01:02.693 --> 00:01:03.693
receptor.

16
00:01:04.133 --> 00:01:06.193
It was built from an activin receptor.

17
00:01:06.993 --> 00:01:09.653
But it also blocks signaling by related ligands.

18
00:01:10.583 --> 00:01:14.163
Cut caps from the injected embryos and culture them alone.

19
00:01:14.883 --> 00:01:15.883
What do they become?

20
00:01:21.817 --> 00:01:25.937
Neural tissue, with no mesoderm detected that could have induced it.

21
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:32.660
Blocking a receptor, not adding a signal, made neural tissue.

22
00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:36.690
Which blocked signal normally keeps ectoderm as skin?

23
00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:40.630
The receptor blocks activin, and related signals too.

24
00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:44.370
But activin added to caps makes mesoderm, not skin.

25
00:01:45.383 --> 00:01:47.963
A relative fit better: BMP4.

26
00:01:48.723 --> 00:01:50.843
Its RNA is in the ectoderm early.

27
00:01:51.883 --> 00:01:55.503
And it is cleared from the dorsal side as the neural plate appears.

28
00:01:56.583 --> 00:02:01.443
To test BMP4 directly, you need cap cells that would otherwise turn neural.

29
00:02:02.483 --> 00:02:06.403
In 1989, Horst Grunz and Lothar Tacke had found them.

30
00:02:07.333 --> 00:02:12.913
They separated cap cells in medium lacking the calcium and magnesium the cells need

31
00:02:12.913 --> 00:02:13.913
to stick together.

32
00:02:14.716 --> 00:02:18.056
They kept the cells apart for hours, then let them clump again.

33
00:02:18.916 --> 00:02:20.196
The cells made neural tissue.

34
00:02:21.266 --> 00:02:27.546
In 1995, Paul Wilson and Hemmati-Brivanlou separated cap cells the same way and

35
00:02:27.546 --> 00:02:29.486
added BMP4 protein.

36
00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:31.890
What do these cells become?

37
00:02:37.933 --> 00:02:41.153
Epidermis. Their neural genes stayed off.

38
00:02:41.973 --> 00:02:44.233
The ligand alone was enough to restore skin.

39
00:02:47.733 --> 00:02:50.893
The takeaway: jam the receptor and a cap turns neural.

40
00:02:51.813 --> 00:02:55.333
Add BMP4 back and separated cells return to skin.
